  "title": "Left on the Ice",
  "subtitle": "The Karluk Disaster and the Canadian Arctic Expedition's Fight for Survival",
  "authorDisplayName": "Liszt Think",
  "description": "In August 1913, the Karluk, flagship of the Canadian Arctic Expedition, became trapped in pack ice off Alaska. When leader Vilhjalmur Stefansson departed on a hunting trip, he never returned to the vessel. Captain Robert Bartlett remained as the ship drifted westward for months before being crushed in January 1914. What followed was a harrowing march across shifting floes to Wrangel Island, where two parties vanished and survivors endured starvation and discord. Bartlett and Inuit hunter Kataktovik then undertook a perilous trek across the Chukchi Sea to Siberia to summon aid. Drawn from diaries, expedition records, and official inquiries, this narrative reconstructs the disaster through the eyes of those who lived it. The documents speak for themselves regarding Stefansson’s controversial departure, illuminating a story of endurance, leadership under duress, and the unforgiving reality of polar exploration.",
